4. Founder asks: 'is unequal vesting a problem?'
==============================================================================
[law / text] hand_back=False actions=[]
Vesting means founders earn their equity over time, so someone who leaves early doesn't walk away with a large part of the company. The standard is 48 months with a 12-month cliff: nothing vests in the first year, a quarter vests at the cliff, then the rest monthly. Different schedules for different founders are legal, but everyone should understand and agree to them. In your case, Alice Chen vests over 48 months with a 12-month cliff; Bob Diaz vests over 24 months with a 6-month cliff. Your 83(b) deadline is **December 31, 2026** (30 days after your incorporation date).

**What I noticed in your case:**
- Your founders are on different vesting schedules (Alice Chen: 48 months with a 12-month cliff; Bob Diaz: 24 months with a 6-month cliff). That is allowed, but make sure everyone agrees to it, because it changes how much equity each founder keeps if they leave early.

This is not legal advice. It was generated automatically; have a licensed attorney review it before you rely on it.
